# ★ Make /dev/socket/thermal-* actually carry vendor_thermal_socket.
#
# OPEN-ISSUES.md #36, filed in session 22 with the note "cannot be VALIDATED
# until SELinux goes enforcing, and applying an unvalidatable relabel to the
# thermal path is a poor trade while the device is permissive". Session 24 went
# enforcing (MALBEC_BRINGUP=1) and it validated itself immediately:
#
#   avc: denied { write } for comm="binder:1647_2" name="thermal-send-client"
#        dev="tmpfs" ino=2150 scontext=u:r:hal_camera_default:s0
#        tcontext=u:object_r:socket_device:s0 tclass=sock_file permissive=0
#
# ★ permissive=0 — a real failure, not a log line. Fifteen of them during ONE
# camera session, which is the camera HAL failing to post thermal notifications
# every time it wanted to.
#
# ── Why the label was never right ─────────────────────────────────────────
#
# thermal-engine-v2 creates these sockets with bind() at runtime, and a
# runtime-created file takes the DIRECTORY's type unless a type_transition says
# otherwise. /dev/socket is socket_device. `file_contexts` only decides labels
# for things that get restorecon'd, and nothing restorecons these — so the
# entries in QTI's own vendor_file_contexts have never applied to anything.
#
# ⚠️ QTI wrote every part of this except the one that makes it work. Already
# present in device/qcom/sepolicy_vndr/sm8750/generic/vendor/common/:
#
#   file.te:128           type vendor_thermal_socket, file_type;
#   thermal-engine.te:46  allow vendor_thermal-engine vendor_thermal_socket:dir w_dir_perms;
#   thermal-engine.te:47  allow vendor_thermal-engine vendor_thermal_socket:sock_file create_file_perms;
#
# and in stock's compiled policy, three client grants against the same type:
#
#   (allow hal_camera            vendor_thermal_socket (sock_file (write)))
#   (allow vendor_qt_service     vendor_thermal_socket (sock_file (write)))
#   (allow vendor_hal_perf_default vendor_thermal_socket (sock_file (write)))
#   + the matching (unix_stream_socket (connectto)) for each
#
# So the type is declared, the creator is allowed to make files of it, and three
# clients are allowed to write it — and **nothing has ever carried it**, on this
# port or on the shipping ZUI image. The whole apparatus is a dead letter one
# statement short of working, and this file is that statement.
#
# ── The creator, established rather than assumed ──────────────────────────
#
#   $ cat /proc/$(pgrep -f thermal-engine-v2)/attr/current
#     u:r:vendor_thermal-engine:s0
#   $ strings /vendor/bin/thermal-engine-v2            | grep -c thermal-send-client  -> 2
#   $ strings .../android.hardware.thermal-service.qti | grep -c thermal-send-client  -> 0
#
# i.e. thermal-engine-v2 owns the names; the QTI thermal HAL (which #41 switched
# to Lenovo's blob) does not create them.
#
# All four names that exist on the device, taken from `ls -lZ /dev/socket/`
# rather than from the stock file_contexts, which lists three:
type_transition vendor_thermal-engine socket_device:sock_file
                vendor_thermal_socket "thermal-send-client";
type_transition vendor_thermal-engine socket_device:sock_file
                vendor_thermal_socket "thermal-recv-client";
type_transition vendor_thermal-engine socket_device:sock_file
                vendor_thermal_socket "thermal-recv-passive-client";
type_transition vendor_thermal-engine socket_device:sock_file
                vendor_thermal_socket "thermal-send-rule";

# The clients. Narrower than stock's, deliberately: stock grants the `hal_camera`
# ATTRIBUTE (server + every client) where the measured denial is the server
# domain alone, and stock's third grantee `vendor_qt_service` is not shipped here
# (`ps -A` has no qt_service).
#
# vendor_hal_perf_default IS shipped (vendor.qti.hardware.perf2-hal-service is
# running) and stock grants it the same pair, so it is included before it produces
# its own denial rather than after.
#
# `connectto` on the creating domain is required as well as `write` on the socket
# file — a unix socket connect() is checked against the peer's domain, not only
# against the inode. Stock pairs them for all three grantees for this reason.
allow hal_camera_default vendor_thermal_socket:sock_file write;
allow hal_camera_default vendor_thermal-engine:unix_stream_socket connectto;

allow vendor_hal_perf_default vendor_thermal_socket:sock_file write;
allow vendor_hal_perf_default vendor_thermal-engine:unix_stream_socket connectto;

# ⚠️ Acceptance test, because a type_transition that does not fire is invisible:
#
#   ls -lZ /dev/socket/thermal-*        all four -> u:object_r:vendor_thermal_socket:s0
#   logcat -b all | grep socket_device  empty after a camera session
#
# If the labels are still socket_device, the transition did not fire — check that
# thermal-engine-v2 really is the creator on that build (the QTI thermal HAL is a
# blob and Lenovo could move the bind() into it).
